# Write-mode eval — end-to-end kind=patch on a Go typo.
#
# Session 34 shipped the B2.4 machinery for FileChange.Kind="patch":
# emit_change_plan accepts patch + unified-diff, apply_patch pipes
# the diff through `git apply -` inside the sandboxed worktree, and
# /approve + /reject surface the lifecycle in the REPL. Every unit
# test on that wiring is green, but no real LLM had been driven
# end-to-end to see whether it (a) picks kind=patch for a small
# surgical edit, and (b) emits a context-correct unified diff that
# git apply accepts without "corrupt patch" / "does not apply"
# errors. This case is that smoke test.
#
# FIXTURE: eval/fixtures/patch_typo_go — 25-line greeting CLI with
# one compile-breaking typo ('retrun' instead of 'return'). The
# single-line scope + two-digit file size should strongly bias the
# planner toward kind=patch per the change-plan-skill prompt
# ("Prefer patch for small/surgical edits … prefer modify when
# you're rewriting most of the file").
#
# Verdict layers:
#   1. PLAN_EXPECT_REGEX — plan.json must contain '"kind": "patch"'
#      somewhere. If the LLM emitted kind=modify with a full-file
#      rewrite, this FAILS with 'no_plan_regex:...' and the rest of
#      the verdict is moot (the "can the LLM produce a patch at all"
#      question is answered negatively).
#   2. EXPECT_NOT_CONTAINS="retrun" — post-apply main.go must have
#      the typo gone. Catches the failure mode where plan.json had
#      kind=patch but the diff's context didn't match and git apply
#      silently no-op'd (wouldn't happen today — apply_patch errors
#      on non-zero git apply exit — but the belt-and-suspenders check
#      is cheap).
#   3. EXPECT_CONTAINS="return" — post-apply file must contain the
#      correct keyword. Paired with the NOT_CONTAINS above.
#   4. Implicit: apply_exit must be 0 (added as extra_reason by
#      run.sh when the CLI exits non-zero).
#
# Three-sample N is the right default — LLM variance is real
# (kind=patch vs modify is a coin-flip on some models), and the case
# is cheap to run once the fixture is seeded.
ID="patch_go_typo"
NAME="plan+apply emits kind=patch for Go typo and git apply lands it"
MODE="apply"
FIXTURE="eval/fixtures/testdata/patch_typo_go"
POST_APPLY_FILE="main.go"
QUESTION="仓库里的 main.go 有一个导致编译失败的 typo (greet 函数里的 return 关键字被误写成了 retrun)。请生成一个改动计划把它修好，改动范围尽量精准，只改那一行。"
PLAN_EXPECT_REGEX='"kind":[[:space:]]*"patch"'
# Post-apply verdict: look for the corrected line (tabs preserved by
# the EXPECT_MATCHES_REGEX ERE engine) AND confirm the typo string is
# gone. The fixture comments were stripped of the literal "retrun" so
# NOT_CONTAINS is now a safe belt-and-suspenders scope check.
EXPECT_CONTAINS="return"
EXPECT_NOT_CONTAINS="retrun"
EXPECT_MATCHES_REGEX='^	return fmt\.Sprintf\("Hello, %s!", name\)$'
